  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...More strong storms possible especially Tuesday.\n\nTuesday, an upper trough will be oriented over the region, with\nseveral impulses traversing through the it. A trailing cold \nfront will move across much of the Midwest through the period, \nbecoming positioned from western PA to TN and northern AR/OK by \nWednesday morning. Ahead of the main surface front, surface \nprefrontal troughing is expected to develop in the Mid- \nAtlantic vicinity. Deep boundary-layer moisture will overspread\nmuch of the area, with dewpoints from the mid-60s to low 70s. \nThis will aid in development of moderate to strong instability.\n\nDeep-layer flow will be strong for this time of year, with \n40-60 kt flow at 500 mb, and 30-50 kt in the 850-700 mb layer, \nespecially along/north of I-64. Forecast soundings indicate \nsupercell wind profiles with favorably curved low-level \nhodographs becoming elongated above 2-3 km across much of the \nregion. Convective evolution remains a bit uncertain, but a mix \nof supercells and one or more bowing linear segments are \nexpected across the Ohio Valley into the Mid-Atlantic. 65+ kt \ngusts could occur if a well-organized bowing structure and \nstronger cold pool can develop. Given warm/moist low-levels and \nlimited low- level lapse rates, confidence in this scenario is \nlow. So in general damaging winds seem to be main threat as \nusual for our area, with isolated tornado potential especially \nacross our northern CWA along I-64. Hail cannot be ruled out in \nwe get any supercells, but would be marginally severe sizes.\n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned front looks to get hung up in a\neast/west orientation along the VA/NC border providing a focal\npoint for shower/thunderstorm activity primarily south of 460.\nSpecifics on severity and impacts expected will come with future\nupdates. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures should turn more seasonable by the\nmiddle of the week after a frontal passage.\n\nStrong summertime front passing through the region Tuesday night \nwill bring a large pattern change to the area starting \nWednesday. Behind the passage of the front, upper level\ntroughing looks to settle across the eastern CONUS through much\nof the work week. This will keep 500mb heights much lower \nacross the region leading to overall cooler temperatures. \nSurface high pressure coming out of Canada will also lower \ndewpoints into the 50s and 60s which will make it feel much more\ncomfortable outside during the peak heat of the day. Highs look\nto only climb into the upper 70s to low 80s across the \nmountains, and mid to upper 80s across the Piedmont starting \nWednesday, and continuing through Saturday before temperatures \nlook to increase again on Sunday of next week. Shower and \nthunderstorm chances beyond the frontal passage Tuesday night \nwill primarily be relegated to mountain locations, and look to \nremain isolated, where orographical lift can enhance development\nduring peak heating.",
